 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n[Bug]: Enum subclass detection fails with certain inheritance patterns\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nWhen working with Enum subclasses, the `_is_enum_subclass` function fails to correctly identify classes that inherit from Enum in certain scenarios. This causes issues with ancestor resolution and can lead to incorrect type inference.\n\n### How to Reproduce\n\nThe issue can be reproduced with the following code:\n\n```python\nimport string\nTemplate = string.Template\n\nclass A(Template):\n    pass\n\nclass B(A):\n    pass\n\ndef test(x=False):\n    if x:\n        string.Template = A\n    else:\n        string.Template = B\n```\n\nWhen analyzing this code, astroid fails to correctly resolve the ancestors for class A. The issue appears to be related to how enum subclasses are detected, particularly when there's a complex inheritance pattern or when classes are being reassigned.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe code should correctly identify all ancestors of class A, including `string.Template`. The ancestor resolution should work properly regardless of the inheritance pattern or reassignments in the code.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nThe ancestor resolution fails, and astroid is unable to correctly identify all ancestors of class A. This leads to incorrect type inference and can cause issues in static analysis tools that rely on astroid.\n\n### Additional context\n\nAfter investigating, the issue appears to be in the `_is_enum_subclass` function in `brain_namedtuple_enum.py`. The current implementation relies on `is_subtype_of` which doesn't handle certain edge cases correctly. The function needs to be more robust when dealing with complex inheritance patterns.\n\n### Python version\n\n3.10\n\n### Astroid version\n\nLatest development version\n</issue>\n",
